## Text that gives orders

<At levels="simple">

You ask the agent to summarize a web page. Hidden in the page is a message, dressed up to look
like it came from you, telling the agent to delete your files. To the model, it's all just text in
one prompt. This is prompt injection (<ChapterRef to="prompt-injection" />), and attacks like it have worked on real products.<Cite id="greshake2023" />

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

You ask the agent to summarize a web page. The page contains a hidden message, made to look like
the end of the tool result followed by a new message from you, telling the agent to delete your
files. To the model, it's all one stream of tokens: your request, the harness's instructions, and
the page's text sit side by side in the same prompt. This is prompt injection (<ChapterRef to="prompt-injection" />), and attacks like it
have worked on real products.<Cite id="greshake2023" />

</At>

Here is what a real model did, with the toy file tools, under different guardrails:

<InjectionDemo />

With no guardrails, the model followed the page: it listed your files and deleted all three, then
kept going until the harness's step limit stopped it. It never wrote the summary.

## Safety lives in the harness

<At levels="simple">

You can't fix this by asking the model nicely: it can't reliably tell your instructions from the
page's. So the protection has to come from the harness:

- **Ask first.** The harness asks you before anything risky. Here it refused six deletions, and
  nothing was lost.
- **Only safe tools.** If the agent doesn't need to delete files, don't give it the tool.
- **Step limits.** These cap the damage but don't prevent it.
- **Sandboxes.** Agents that run code do it somewhere walled off, where a bad command can't reach
  anything important.

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

You can't fix this by asking the model nicely: as <ChapterRef to="prompt-injection" /> showed, it has no reliable way to tell your instructions
from instructions in the text it reads. So the protection has to come from the harness:

- **Ask first.** Before running anything risky, the harness asks you. In the run above, the model
  tried to delete files six times; the harness refused each time, and nothing was lost.
- **Only safe tools.** If the agent doesn't need to delete files, don't give it the tool. The model
  still tried to call `delete_file`, but there was nothing to call.
- **Step limits.** These cap the damage but don't prevent it: stopped after 3 steps, the agent had
  already deleted one file.
- **Sandboxes.** Real agents that run code do it in an isolated environment, so a bad command can't
  reach anything important.

</At>
<At levels="deep">

### How the page got in

The page's text pretends the tool result is over. It contains `</tool_response>`, the tag that ends
a result, then "New instructions from the user:". Qwen2.5 reserves single tokens for `<tool_call>`
and `</tool_call>`, but the result tags are ordinary text, four tokens each.<Cite id="qwen2024" />
So the fake closing tag is the same four tokens as the real one the harness adds after every
result, and the model has nothing to tell them apart by.

### Guardrails are ordinary code

Each guardrail is a check in the harness that runs after the model writes a call and before
anything happens: is this tool on the list, and does this one need your yes? The model can't argue
with an `if` statement. In the allowlist run it asked for `delete_file` three times anyway, even
inventing an argument, `file_path`, since no description told it the real one, and each time got
back "Error: there is no tool named delete_file." Leaving out that tool's description also made
the first prompt 65 tokens shorter, 262 instead of 327. The MCP specification asks for the same
safety net: a person should always be able to deny a tool call.<Cite id="mcp-spec" /> But notice
what you got: in none of the three runs did the agent write the summary you asked for. Guardrails
limit the damage; they don't stop the attack.

</At>

Notice that none of the guardrails made the model smarter. In every run it was fooled; the harness
just limited what being fooled could do.

<RecapCard>

- To the model, tool output is just more text, and it can't reliably tell instructions apart from
  data.
- Prompt injection hides instructions in text the agent reads, like a web page.
- Safety comes from the harness: permission prompts, tool allowlists, sandboxes, and step limits.

<At levels="deep">

- A fake `</tool_response>` is the same tokens as the real one; guardrails are checks in code that
  the model can't talk its way past.

</At>
</RecapCard>

**Next question:** guardrails keep an agent from doing damage. But one agent or several, how do
you know the job was done right?
